    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi, all<div><br></div><div>I am a undergrad student currently working with some butterfly wing .tps data. My problem is that when I use the function fixed.angle it keeps sending me back to estimate.missing, but I have already estimated the missing values and a new data matrix was formed. Is there something that might be a reason for this?</div><div><br></div><div>ex.) </div><div><br></div><div><div>   fixed.angle(postesqmp, <a href=\"http://art.pt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://art.pt&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792485019111000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Ut_kqOOb7lGdgHBWeNuK2\">art.pt</a> = 1, angle.pts.1 = c(8,9), angle.pts = c(2,3), rot.pts = 6,7,8)</div><div>Error in fixed.angle(postesqmp, <a href=\"http://art.pt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://art.pt&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792485019111000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Ut_kqOOb7lGdgHBWeNuK2\">art.pt</a> = 1, angle.pts.1 = c(8, 9), angle.pts = c(2,  : </div><div>  Data matrix 1 contains missing values. Estimate these first(see &#39;estimate.missing&#39;).</div></div><div><br></div><div>I am attaching my script.</div><div><br></div><div>Gratefully, </div><div><br></div><div>Diniz F.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
